1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,657
Tempo Zones are private blockchains anchored to Tempo.

2
00:00:03,707 --> 00:00:10,145
Today, the check that a Zone batch was executed correctly is a hardware attestation,

3
00:00:10,195 --> 00:00:13,852
or, in the reference contract, nothing at all.

4
00:00:13,902 --> 00:00:18,950
Tempo's own docs say zero-knowledge proving for Zones is not implemented.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:20,768
So I built it.

6
00:00:20,818 --> 00:00:25,768
Sworn runs Tempo's own Zone batch verifier inside a zero-knowledge VM,

7
00:00:25,818 --> 00:00:33,950
binds the proof to the exact inputs Tempo's verifier takes, and a contract on Tempo's testnet verified it.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:35,768
Here is the transaction.

9
00:00:35,818 --> 00:00:38,495
The same engine re-runs Tempo's EVM,

10
00:00:38,545 --> 00:00:43,950
and it has already slashed a lying server three times on Moderato.

11
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,521
Zones hold money, and money has to come back out.

12
00:00:48,571 --> 00:00:53,550
Withdrawals are only as trustworthy as the check on the batch.

13
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:59,950
A hardware attestation means trusting one vendor's chip; a zero-knowledge proof, anyone can check.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,470
Tempo decides which verifier its Zones use, so step one is Tempo:

15
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:10,070
ZK as a second, independent check next to the attestation.

16
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:13,750
Step two is the business: running the provers,

17
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:19,730
with a proof for every batch, on time, re-verified at every Tempo upgrade.

18
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:22,950
Specialist work that grows with every Zone.

19
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,365
Tempo is built on Reth and Revm, the stack I work in and teach in rethlab,

20
00:01:30,415 --> 00:01:35,926
and my last project won a Uniswap Foundation prize at ETHGlobal Tokyo.

21
00:01:35,976 --> 00:01:41,950
Getting Tempo's code into a zkVM meant patching it, which takes reading it.

22
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:47,536
It's testnet and unaudited, the batch comes from Tempo's own integration tests,

23
00:01:47,586 --> 00:01:50,329
and there is no revenue yet.

24
00:01:50,379 --> 00:01:53,588
Next: running both checks together, with Tempo.

25
00:01:53,638 --> 00:01:55,450
Sworn: Tempo's execution, proven.
